---

## Context

provisionBrain plans the brain-layer scaffolding the AIDE pipeline
needs on first install. The framework's brain contract is exactly two
required entry-point artifacts — a playbook entry-point artifact and
a research entry-point artifact — plus the MCP wiring that connects
the host's agents to whatever knowledge store backs them.
provisionBrain's job is to plan five InitStep items that together
stand up that contract: the user-editable `brain.aide` config file,
the brain root directory tree, the playbook entry-point artifact, the
research entry-point artifact, and the brain MCP server entry. It
writes nothing to disk — every step is a planning artifact the install
service's apply layer turns into bytes.

The load-bearing decision is where the byte-level content for those
artifacts comes from. The legacy module owned the launcher shape, the
playbook entry-point body, and the brain-root CLAUDE.md body as inline
TypeScript constants in `index.ts`. The new shape moves all of that
content into the user's own `brain.aide` body and reads it back
through `parseBrainAide`. The default scaffolded `brain.aide` ships
three named marker-bounded body sections — a `prose` section
(agent-facing usage instructions, delimited by
`<!-- aide-prose-start -->` / `<!-- aide-prose-end -->`), a `playbook`
section (the bytes scaffolded as the playbook entry-point artifact,
delimited by `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-playbook-end -->`), and a `research` section (the bytes
scaffolded as the research entry-point artifact, delimited by
`<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `<!-- aide-research-end -->`) — and
the parser exposes each section as its own typed field. provisionBrain
reads `playbook` and `research` to populate the entry-point artifact
steps; the package no longer owns the bytes those steps deliver.

The brain.aide path lives under `.aide/config/`, the host's user-owned
configuration directory established by the root spec. The ownership
rule is directory-wide: any file scaffolded into `.aide/config/` is
owned by the user the moment it lands, and neither aide_init nor
aide_upgrade overwrites, patches, or migrates anything inside that
directory on subsequent runs. brain.aide is the current inhabitant; the
convention is forward-compatible with future user-owned config files,
and provisionBrain inherits the structural rule rather than restating
a per-file invariant.

provisionBrain is also the one-place migration surface for the fixed
`brain` MCP key. Legacy installs that wrote the entry under the old
`obsidian` key are migrated here and nowhere else: when a legacy
`obsidian`-only entry is present, the MCP step reports
`would-overwrite` with key `brain`, so the next `/aide:upgrade` or
`/aide:init` moves the entry. The both-keys-present transitional state
is handled the same way. There is no runtime fallback to the legacy
key elsewhere in the codebase.

The framework's brain contract does NOT include a brain-root
CLAUDE.md. A previous shape returned a CLAUDE.md scaffolding step;
that step has been retired and the framework's brain contract is
exactly two required entry-point artifacts (playbook + research) —
nothing else. Users who want a CLAUDE.md inside their brain are free
to add one — it is their brain — but the framework does not require
it, and provisionBrain emits no InitStep for it. Reintroducing a
CLAUDE.md scaffolding step under any name — "brain-root CLAUDE.md",
"navigation entry-point", or any other rename — restores the retired
contract and is rejected. (The token "vault" appears here only to
forbid the retired naming, per the root spec's anti-example
carve-out.) The methodology guidance that used to live in that file
(link-crawling protocols, decision protocols, where-to-find-things
tables) lives in pipeline prompts, where the strategist owns the
prose, and is delivered to agents through the methodology doc surface
rather than baked into install code.

## Strategy

**Five planning steps in fixed order: brain config → brain root
directories → playbook entry-point artifact → research entry-point
artifact → MCP entry.** Each is its own InitStep with its own
idempotency check, and the order is load-bearing: brain config is
planned first because every step downstream of it derives content
from the bytes it scaffolds. The entry-point artifact steps come
before the MCP step because the parser pass that powers both reads
the same brain.aide; running it once after the brain config step is
resolved keeps the orchestrator's flow linear.

**brain.aide is the single source of truth for both launcher bytes
and entry-point artifact bytes.** The load-bearing structural change
is that the default scaffolded brain.aide ships three named
marker-bounded body sections — `prose` (agent-facing usage
instructions), `playbook` (the playbook entry-point artifact body),
and `research` (the research entry-point artifact body) — and every
artifact step downstream sources its `content` from the corresponding
section via parseBrainAide. The MCP step calls parseBrainAide (or its
in-memory string sibling for the cold-install path where brain.aide
has not yet hit disk) and then `interpolateArgs` to compute the
entry's command and args. The entry-point artifact steps call the
same parser and read `playbook` / `research` for their bytes. No
artifact step hardcodes a launcher shape, a playbook structure, or a
research structure as an inline TypeScript constant — every byte the
apply layer eventually writes for these artifacts traces back to a
body section the user owns and can edit. A user edit to any section
flows into the next install or sync automatically; the package no
longer competes with the user for ownership of any of these bytes.

**Body sections are marker-bounded with a closed marker set; parsed
fields are `prose`, `playbook`, and `research`.** The parser
enforces three required marker pairs in fixed order:
`<!-- aide-prose-start -->` / `<!-- aide-prose-end -->`,
`<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-playbook-end -->`, and
`<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `<!-- aide-research-end -->` —
recognized lowercase and case-sensitive in the prose-then-playbook-
then-research order. The parsed result on the `ok` branch carries
each section as its own typed field whose name matches the marker
token base name verbatim. Marker layout violations (a missing pair, a
typo'd token, an unmatched closer, an opener without a closer, wrong
section order, nested markers) return `malformed-body` naming the
violating marker — there is no recovery, no auto-fill, no silent
default. Bytes outside any marker pair are silently ignored by the
parser; users may keep notes-to-self or scratch text in those
regions. The closed marker set means provisionBrain reaches for the
section it owns by name and never re-implements body grammar.

**The bundled-default-brain.aide template helper keeps its current
name and folder, but its responsibilities expand.** The helper at
`obsidianBrainAideTemplate/` now owns three things: the collapsed
two-field frontmatter, the platform-branched launcher invocation
(POSIX `npx ...` vs Windows `cmd /c npx ...`), AND the full byte
content of all three marker-bounded body sections including the
playbook section bytes and research section bytes. The name remains
intentional because the scaffolded template content is
Obsidian-flavored — the prose names the `mcp__brain__read_note` and
`mcp__brain__search_notes` tools, the playbook and research section
bodies are written as Markdown for an editor-backed brain — and the
helper describes its content, not a code-level special case. The
helper folder name is one of the three explicit forbidden-token
carve-outs in the root spec: it names a non-branching content anchor,
not a dispatch axis.

**Code identifiers and inline prose stay storage-agnostic.** No
identifier in `index.ts` — function name, variable name, parameter
name, file path constant, JSDoc word, log string — uses any of the
forbidden tokens (`vault`, `obsidian`, `note`-as-synonym, `wikilink`,
`hub note`, `hub`). The two structural concepts the module handles
are "playbook entry-point artifact" and "research entry-point
artifact". How those manifest in the user's chosen storage is
brain.aide's concern, not this module's. The bundled default
brain.aide stays Obsidian-flavored because that is the most common
storage choice, but the mental model the code expresses is the
framework's storage-agnostic structural shape, not any one
connector's specific shape.

**Storage-agnostic vocabulary across this spec, the module's code,
and every byte it writes into an InitStep.** The module's prose,
outcomes, comments, JSDoc, log strings, error messages, and InitStep
`name` fields all describe the brain in storage-agnostic terms. The
two allowed exceptions are scoped exactly: the
`obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper folder name (a content anchor for
the bundled template, not a code-level branch target) and the legacy
`obsidian` MCP key referenced inside migration branches (the key
being migrated away from). The `.obsidian` directory heuristic
comment in `brainRootExists` is a third allowed exception inherited
from the root spec but lives in a sibling helper, not in this
module's code. Anti-example occurrences inside undesired-outcome
blocks of this spec are also allowed — naming a forbidden token to
forbid it is the mechanism, not a violation. Every other appearance
of the forbidden tokens, in code or prose, is a regression. The
default scaffolded brain.aide ships an Obsidian-flavored body
because that is the most common storage choice, but that is a
template-content fact (a user-owned byte the moment it lands), not a
code-identifier fact.

**The legacy `obsidianMcpEntry()` export is removed.** That function
returned a hardcoded launcher object directly. Callers that needed it
now go through the template + `parseBrainAide` + `interpolateArgs`
pipeline. Consumers that previously imported `obsidianMcpEntry`
migrate to derive the entry from a scaffolded or existing brain.aide.

**brain.aide scaffolding is seed-semantic, and the rule is
directory-wide.** The brain.aide file is user-owned the moment it
lands on disk — the user may swap the launcher, retarget the inline
path, rewrite the prose, edit the playbook or research section
bodies, or change the label. A presence check at the expected path
is the correct idempotency signal. The module never returns
`would-overwrite` for brain.aide; doing so would replace a user's
customized config with the package's default on every subsequent
init. The same rule applies to every other file under `.aide/config/`
should one ever be added: the directory itself is the ownership
boundary, inherited from the root spec, with no per-file allowlist.

**Two different idempotency semantics coexist in this module.** The
brain config file, the playbook entry-point artifact, and the
research entry-point artifact use **seed-semantic idempotency**: a
simple presence check. If the file exists at its expected path, the
step is `exists` — no byte comparison, no drift detection against the
brain.aide section that originated it. These files are seeds the
user evolves the moment the brain comes alive; once they own the
file, the bytes belong to them, and the package never proposes
replacing them. The brain MCP prescription uses **canonical-derived
idempotency**: the expected entry is computed from the current
brain.aide and compared against what is in `.mcp.json`. Divergence
means `would-overwrite` (the user may have re-run init before
syncing, or the brain.aide was edited), and the migration branches
for legacy `obsidian`-only and both-keys states fire on the same
path. The distinguishing question is who owns the bytes after first
write: for user-curated content, presence is enough; for the derived
MCP entry under `.mcp.json["brain"]`, the current brain.aide is the
source of truth.

**Migration semantics for existing installs.** Cold installs land in
the new shape automatically — the bundled template carries all three
marker-bounded body sections from day one. Existing installs whose
brain.aide carries the pre-pivot heading-based body (no marker pairs
present) will surface `malformed-body` from parseBrainAide on the
first run that requires the new sections, because the parser's body
grammar is closed and missing required marker pairs are a structured
failure naming the missing markers. The remediation is for the user
to hand-edit their brain.aide to wrap each section in its marker
pair; provisionBrain does not silently patch the file under
`.aide/config/` to add them, because that would violate the
directory-wide ownership rule. There is no aide_upgrade carve-out for
this transition; the strict-failure path is intentional.

**Signature takes `projectRoot`, not just `brainPath` and
`mcpConfigPath`.** The pipeline needs the host project root to derive
the brain.aide path at `.aide/config/brain.aide`. Separating
`brainPath` (the brain root) from the project root (where brain.aide
lives) makes the module's contract explicit. The install orchestrator
passes all three values and the single caller update is mechanical.

## Good examples

A cold install on a fresh project. provisionBrain returns five
InitStep items in order:

**Step 1 — Brain config.** Status: would-create. Category: brain.
Name: "Brain config (brain.aide)". FilePath:
`.aide/config/brain.aide` inside the host project root. Content: the
full canonical brain.aide bytes from the bundled template helper,
which on POSIX looks like:

```
---
name: obsidian
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "/home/user/brain"
---

<!-- aide-prose-start -->
Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools.
Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their brain-root-relative
path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries across the
brain. The brain's playbook entry-point artifact is at
`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`; the research entry-point
artifact is at `research/research.md`. Read either entry-point first,
then follow the references it lists into the rest of the brain. ...
<!-- aide-prose-end -->

<!-- aide-playbook-start -->
# Coding Playbook

## Task Routing

| Task domain | Section |
|-------------|---------|

## How to Use This Index

Read this entry-point first. Each section lists its files with
keywords. Drill into the section that matches your task. ...
<!-- aide-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-research-start -->
# Research

## Domains

| Domain | Files |
|--------|-------|

## How to Use This Index

Research material is filed by domain. Drill into the domain that
matches your task; do not flat-search across every domain. ...
<!-- aide-research-end -->
```

(On Windows the `mcpServerConfig` block becomes `command: cmd` with
`args: ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-brain"]`.)
The frontmatter has exactly two top-level fields: `name` (a label
only; the package never branches on it) and `mcpServerConfig`. The
brain root path is inlined byte-for-byte in `args` — no `${rootPath}`
placeholder, no separate `rootPath` field, no `entryFile`, no `tools`
block. The body has exactly three marker-bounded sections in the
required prose-then-playbook-then-research order, each opened and
closed by its own paired HTML-comment markers. Bytes outside any
marker pair (a blank line between frontmatter and the first opener,
spacing between sections) are silently ignored by the parser. Any
marker layout violation — missing pair, typo'd token, unmatched
closer, wrong section order, nested markers — would surface as
`malformed-body` from the parser naming the offending marker.

**Step 2 — Brain root directories.** Status: would-create. Category:
brain. FilePath: the brain root. Content: a JSON array of the
directory paths to create.

**Step 3 — Playbook entry-point artifact.** Status: would-create.
Category: brain. FilePath: the playbook entry-point artifact's path
inside the brain root (e.g., `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` for
an editor-backed Markdown brain — the storage shape is dictated by
the user's chosen backend, not by this module). Content: sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).playbook` — the
verbatim bytes between `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` and
`<!-- aide-playbook-end -->` in the brain.aide that Step 1 just
planned. The bytes are NOT a copy of an inline TypeScript constant
in this module; they came from the brain.aide playbook section, full
stop.

**Step 4 — Research entry-point artifact.** Status: would-create.
Category: brain. FilePath: the research entry-point artifact's path
inside the brain root (e.g., `research/research.md` for an
editor-backed Markdown brain). Content: sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).research` — the
verbatim bytes between `<!-- aide-research-start -->` and
`<!-- aide-research-end -->`. Same parser pass as Step 3, different
field, different artifact path.

**Step 5 — MCP config (brain).** Status: would-create. Category:
mcp. Prescription derived from the same Step 1 content bytes:
parseBrainAide parses the template string, interpolateArgs walks
`mcpServerConfig.args` (which contains no `${...}` placeholders on
the default scaffold, so the args pass through unchanged), and the
resulting entry on POSIX is:

```
{
  command: "npx",
  args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "/home/user/brain"]
}
```

(On Windows: `{ command: "cmd", args: ["/c", "npx",
"@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-brain"] }`.) The args list is
byte-identical to what the template wrote to disk — interpolateArgs
runs but has nothing to substitute on the default scaffold.

Re-running on the same project after the first install: all five
steps return `exists`. The brain.aide presence check fires (the file
is on disk under `.aide/config/`); the brain root directories are
present; the playbook and research entry-point artifacts are present
(presence-only check, no byte comparison against the current
brain.aide section); the MCP config `brain` key matches the derived
entry.

Running on a brain where the user has hand-edited their brain.aide
to retarget the brain root by changing the inline path string in
`mcpServerConfig.args`: the brain config step is `exists` (the file
is present under `.aide/config/`); the brain root directory step
reports based on the new path; the MCP step derives its expected
entry from the user's edited brain.aide, NOT from the bundled
template default — the user's customization flows through
automatically. The entry-point artifacts at the new brain root either
exist (if the user already moved them) and report `exists`, or are
absent and report `would-create` with content derived from the user's
current `playbook` and `research` body sections. An advanced user who
instead pulls the path into a (now custom) top-level frontmatter
field and references it in `args` via `${myPath}` exercises the
`${...}` interpolation surface; `interpolateArgs` substitutes the
value and the derived entry's args carry the resolved string.

A migration run on a legacy install (entry previously written under
`obsidian` key, no `brain` key): the MCP step reports
`would-overwrite` with `prescription: { key: "brain", entry: <derived
from brain.aide> }`. The other four steps run their normal
idempotency checks. After apply, the legacy `obsidian` key is
replaced by the canonical `brain` key. The both-keys-present
transitional state takes the same `would-overwrite` branch with the
same prescription.

## Bad examples

A provisionBrain that hardcodes entry-point artifact bodies as inline
TypeScript constants:

```
// Wrong — entry-point artifact bytes outside the brain.aide body
const PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE = `# Coding Playbook\n\n## Task Routing...`;
const RESEARCH_TEMPLATE = `# Research\n\n## Domains...`;

steps.push({ name: "Playbook entry-point", content: PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE });
steps.push({ name: "Research entry-point", content: RESEARCH_TEMPLATE });
```

The package now owns two top-level inline TypeScript constants in
`index.ts` (one per entry-point artifact). A user who edits their
brain.aide's `playbook` or `research` body section to add their own
routing rows or domain entries finds the next install ignores their
edit and lands the package default again. This is the same regression
as the legacy launcher-bytes duplication the schema collapse already
retired, applied to a different kind of byte. Entry-point artifact
bytes belong in the named marker-bounded brain.aide body sections and
nowhere else in this module.

A provisionBrain that scaffolds a brain-root CLAUDE.md as part of the
brain contract:

```
// Wrong — CLAUDE.md is no longer a framework requirement
steps.push({
  name: "Brain-root CLAUDE.md",
  filePath: join(brainPath, "CLAUDE.md"),
  content: BRAIN_CLAUDE_MD_TEMPLATE, // navigation entry-point bytes
});
```

The framework's brain contract is exactly two required entry-point
artifacts (playbook + research). A brain-root CLAUDE.md is a user
choice, not a framework requirement, and the navigation methodology
that used to live in that file (link-crawling protocols, decision
protocols, where-to-find-things tables) is now delivered through
pipeline prompts. Reintroducing a CLAUDE.md scaffolding step under
any name — "brain-root CLAUDE.md", "navigation entry-point",
"navigation hub" — restores the retired contract. Users who want a
CLAUDE.md in their brain add one themselves; the package does not
plan one. (The token "vault" appears nowhere here because the retired
naming has been swept; the prohibition still holds against any rename
that would reintroduce a CLAUDE.md scaffolding step.)

A provisionBrain that uses storage-specific identifiers in code:

```
// Wrong — connector-specific tokens in code identifiers
async function vaultExists(brainPath: string): Promise<boolean> { ... }
const VAULT_DIRS = ["research", "process/retro", "coding-playbook"];
const obsidianHubPath = join(brainPath, "coding-playbook", "coding-playbook.md");
```

The mental model these names express is one connector's, not the
framework's. A user whose brain is a hosted knowledge base, a plain
folder, or any storage other than the default still needs the
playbook entry-point artifact and research entry-point artifact to
land somewhere; the code that plans those landings should not assume
the landing is a `.md` file inside an editor-backed brain. The
structural concepts the module handles are "brain root", "playbook
entry-point artifact", and "research entry-point artifact" — how
those manifest in the user's chosen storage is brain.aide's concern,
expressed in the body sections the user owns. The bundled default
template can be Obsidian-flavored because that is the most common
storage choice, but no identifier or string constant in `index.ts`
may name any of the forbidden tokens. Helpers that retain the retired
storage-specific tokens in their names — and any helper named with
those tokens whether as `*Exists`, a `*_DIRS` constant, or a
`*HubPath` derivation — are the same regression in different clothing
and must be renamed to the storage-agnostic shape the module's code
already uses elsewhere.

A provisionBrain that embeds link-crawling navigation prose as an
inline constant:

```
// Wrong — methodology guidance baked into install code
const NAVIGATION_PROSE = `When reading any entry-point file, follow
the inline references it lists that are relevant to your task.
Entry-point files are navigation; content files are where depth
starts...`;
```

Link-crawling protocols, entry-point-vs-content depth rules, decision
protocols ("use the study-playbook skill for X, search-notes for Y"),
and where-to-find-things tables are methodology guidance that lives
in pipeline prompts, not in install code. Baking it into a constant
in `index.ts` couples the install pipeline to a specific brain
navigation paradigm and forces every methodology revision to ship as
a code release. The strategist owns this prose; the install module
is allowed to plan that entry-point artifacts land on disk and to
source their bytes from brain.aide, but it does not own the
navigation methodology itself.

A provisionBrain that skips the brain.aide step and constructs the
MCP entry inline:

```
// Wrong — reconstructs launcher directly
const entry = obsidianMcpEntry(brainPath);
steps.push({ name: "MCP config (brain)", prescription: { key: "brain", entry } });
```

The user's brain.aide (which may have a custom launcher, a different
label, or a different inline path) is ignored. The package's compiled
constant wins every time, defeating the user-ownership model the
refactor was built around.

A provisionBrain that hardcodes the Windows launcher shape in
index.ts:

```
// Wrong — launcher bytes outside the template helper
const command = process.platform === "win32" ? "cmd" : "npx";
const args = process.platform === "win32"
  ? ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", brainPath]
  : ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", brainPath];
```

The canonical launcher bytes now live in two places. When the
template helper changes (say, a new flag is added to the args list,
or the default launcher is swapped out for something else), this
inline copy silently diverges and the scaffolded brain.aide no
longer matches what the MCP step would compute for a cold install.
The same rejection applies to any inline copy of the legacy
`obsidian-mcp` launcher — duplicating launcher bytes outside the
template is the bug, not the specific launcher being duplicated.

A provisionBrain that returns `would-overwrite` for the seed
brain.aide:

```
// Wrong — byte-compares a user-owned config file
const disk = await readFile(brainAidePath, "utf8");
const template = bundledBrainAideTemplate(brainPath);
if (disk !== template) {
  return { name: "Brain config (brain.aide)", status: "would-overwrite", content: template };
}
```

The user edited their brain.aide to point at a new brain root. On
the next init, they are asked "overwrite your brain config?" A
reflexive Enter replaces their custom inline path with the stale
default from the template. This is data loss from a pattern that
only looks safe — and the same failure mode applies to any future
file scaffolded under `.aide/config/`: the directory boundary is the
ownership signal, and no code path in this module is allowed to
return `would-overwrite` for any path inside it.

A provisionBrain that scaffolds a default brain.aide using
`${rootPath}` substitution:

```
// Wrong — default scaffold exercises the advanced-user interpolation surface
return bundledBrainAideTemplate(brainPath); // emits args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "${rootPath}"]
```

The default scaffold's job is to produce a working brain config that
the user can read top-to-bottom without learning a templating DSL.
Substitution is the opt-in path for advanced users who want to DRY a
value across positions; baking it into the default forces every user
into the advanced workflow before they have ever touched their
config.

A brain.aide scaffolded with intent-spec frontmatter fields:

```
---
scope: .aide/config/brain.aide # Wrong — no scope in brain.aide
intent: configures the brain   # Wrong — no intent paragraph
outcomes:                      # Wrong — no outcomes block
  desired:
    - brain is wired
name: obsidian
...
```

Every downstream agent that reads this file now has to decide which
rules apply — the brain.aide config rules or the intent-spec rules.
The distinction between config files and intent specs is load-bearing
and must be enforced at the scaffolding layer.

A brain.aide scaffolded with extra frontmatter fields beyond the
collapsed schema:

```
---
name: obsidian
connector: obsidian            # Wrong — field renamed away; `name` is the only label
rootPath: D:/notes/my-brain    # Wrong — path lives inline in args, not as its own field
entryFile: CLAUDE.md           # Wrong — no code consumes this
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "${rootPath}"]
tools:                         # Wrong — interface contract lives in the prose body
  read: mcp__brain__read_note
  search: mcp__brain__search_notes
---
```

Each removed field is validation theater — a slot the user is nagged
to fill in with no code path that consumes the value once filled.
The schema collapse is load-bearing: every frontmatter field must
justify itself by naming a real runtime consumer (sync writes
`mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as a label). The
agent-facing interface (which MCP tool names to call, where to start
reading, how to follow links) lives in the prose body where the
agent actually reads it.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` (frontmatter and broader brain-layer outcomes)
  — Parent spec: brain.aide as the single source of truth for brain
  configuration, the collapsed two-field schema (`name` +
  `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`), the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`
  default launcher, the `.aide/config/` directory ownership boundary,
  the sync contract as the only mutation surface for `.mcp.json`,
  the no-substitution-in-the-default-scaffold rule, and the
  outcomes.undesired rule that brain.aide must not borrow intent-spec
  frontmatter conventions or add validation-theater fields. Informed
  the directory-wide ownership invariant, the schema-minimum-equals-
  maximum rule, and the no-connector-branching constraint that drives
  storage-agnostic identifier naming in this module.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (outcome on the body schema) — Parent spec:
  established that the body of `brain.aide` is parsed as exactly
  three named sections (`prose`, `playbook`, `research`) delimited
  by paired HTML-comment markers recognized lowercase and case-
  sensitive in the required prose-then-playbook-then-research order,
  with marker layout violations halting the pipeline as
  `malformed-body` naming the offending marker and bytes outside any
  marker pair silently ignored. Drove the marker-bounded body
  vocabulary across this spec's Strategy, the worked Good example,
  and the Bad-example regression scenarios.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (outcome on storage-agnostic vocabulary) —
  Parent spec: established the forbidden-token list (`vault`,
  `obsidian`, `note`-as-synonym, `wikilink`, `hub note`, `hub`) and
  the rule that those tokens must not appear in any user-facing
  prose surface or in code identifiers, comments, or string
  constants project-wide. Drove the Strategy beat on storage-
  agnostic vocabulary in this spec, the field-name shift to
  `playbook` and `research` (away from the retired suffixed forms),
  the Bad-example rewrite of identifier-naming and link-crawling
  scenarios, and the anti-example carve-out scoping (anti-example
  narratives that explicitly forbid a token are allowed; generic
  prose stays clean).
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` — Sibling spec: the parser that
  reads brain.aide and returns typed frontmatter plus three named
  body fields (`prose`, `playbook`, `research`) extracted from
  paired HTML-comment markers, plus the interpolateArgs helper that
  walks `mcpServerConfig.args` substituting `${...}` references
  against frontmatter values. provisionBrain consumes parseBrainAide
  for both the MCP step's prescription AND the byte content of the
  playbook entry-point and research entry-point artifact steps —
  every artifact's `content` field traces back through this parser
  to a brain.aide body section, never to an inline TypeScript
  constant. Informed the strict-failure migration semantics for
  existing installs whose brain.aide carries the pre-pivot
  heading-based body (parseBrainAide returns `malformed-body`
  naming the missing markers).
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/` —
  Sibling helper: the bundled default brain.aide template. Now owns
  the collapsed two-field frontmatter, the platform-branched
  launcher invocation, AND the byte content of all three
  marker-bounded body sections (`prose`, `playbook`, `research`).
  Informed the strategy decision that this helper's responsibilities
  expand to include the playbook and research section bodies, and
  that the helper folder name remains one of the explicit
  forbidden-token carve-outs (a non-branching content anchor for the
  bundled template, not a dispatch axis).
- `.aide/handoff.aide` — Post-collapse architectural snapshot:
  documents this module as the focus of intents #6 (entry-point
  artifact bytes flow from brain.aide via parseBrainAide) and #8
  (the 5-step pipeline order, with steps 3 and 4 sourcing content
  from the brain.aide planned by step 1). Informed the preservation
  of the five-step pipeline structure verbatim through this
  vocabulary resync, and confirmed the post-collapse identifier-
  naming sweep already landed in code (`vaultExists` →
  `brainRootExists`, `VAULT_DIRS` → `BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS`) so the
  Bad-example identifier scenario stays as a regression-against-
  rollback rather than a description of current code.
- `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` — Methodology doc: brain.aide file
  format, frontmatter field semantics, the marker-bounded body
  section vocabulary, the two substitution surfaces (args at
  install time, body sections verbatim at agent and install time),
  and the divergence from intent-spec conventions that makes
  scope/intent/outcomes fields explicitly absent.
